// Practice quiz: Reading: API gateways and service discovery
// Unreviewed first draft, 2026-11-04. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: containers-cicd/microservices-and-ci-cd/api-gateways-and-service-discovery

::o1 what::A web app and a mobile app use five services. What does an API gateway give them? {
=One entry point that routes each request to the right service#Correct. Clients are decoupled from the internal layout.
~A shared database#Not quite. Gateways route requests; they are not data stores.
~A faster CPU#Not quite. A gateway is one more hop, not extra compute.
~A replacement for all five services#Not quite. The services still do the work.
}

::o1 concern::Which of these is a typical shared concern to handle at a gateway? {
~An order's pricing rules#Not quite. That is business logic, which belongs in a service.
=Authentication#Correct. Offloading it avoids repeating it in each service.
~A service's database schema#Not quite. Each service owns its own schema.
~Compiling service code#Not quite. That happens at build time, in CI.
}

::o2 client side::In client-side discovery, who picks the service instance? {
~A load balancer the caller sends to#Not quite. That is server-side discovery.
~The database#Not quite. A database does not route service calls.
=The caller, after querying the registry#Correct. The caller does the lookup and the choice.
~The Git repository#Not quite. That has no part in runtime routing.
}

::o2 server side::Which is an advantage of server-side discovery? {
~No extra component is needed#Not quite. It needs a router or load balancer.
~Each language needs a library#Not quite. That is the client-side burden.
~Instances never change address#Not quite. Discovery exists because they do change.
=Client code stays simple, because a router handles the lookup#Correct. Callers only send to a well-known address.
}

::o3 compose::Why should a Compose service call http\://cache\:6379 and not a container IP address? {
=The name stays stable while container addresses can change#Correct. Docker assigns addresses dynamically.
~IP addresses are forbidden in URLs#Not quite. They are allowed, just unreliable here.
~Names are faster than IPs#Not quite. Speed is not the reason.
~DNS encrypts the call#Not quite. DNS does not encrypt anything.
}
